.TH DROP-ZONE 1 "September 2026" "drop-zone 1.0.0" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
drop-zone \- send files straight to another person's terminal
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B drop-zone
.I COMMAND
.RI [ OPTION ...]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B drop-zone
sends files directly between two machines. A receiver runs
.B accept
and a sender runs
.B send .
The rendezvous server introduces the two peers and then steps out of the way:
it never sees a password, a filename or a file byte, and it keeps no record of
either peer.
.PP
The transfer uses the best path the two machines can open: a direct TCP
connection, a hole-punched UDP stream, or, if both of those fail, a relay of
ciphertext through the server.
.SH COMMANDS
.TP
.B setup
Choose a username and passwords, and create an identity for this machine.
Writes
.I ~/.config/drop-zone/config.toml
and a sealed
.I identity.key
with mode 0600.
.TP
.B accept
Wait for incoming files and save them. Prompts before each transfer unless
.B \-y
is given.
.TP
.B send
.I FILE...
.B \-t
.I USER
Send files or directories to
.IR USER .
.TP
.B whoami
Print this machine's username and identity fingerprint.
.TP
.B status
Print the configuration and what this build can do, including which AEAD the
CPU will use.
.SH OPTIONS
.SS Send options
.TP
.BR \-t ", " \-\-target = USER
Username to send to.
.TP
.BR \-p ", " \-\-password = PASSWORD
.IR USER 's
public password. Omit it and drop-zone will ask, which keeps it out of the
shell history.
.SS Accept options
.TP
.BR \-o ", " \-\-output = DIR
Directory to save files in. Default: the directory the command was run from.
.TP
.BR \-y ", " \-\-yes
Accept every transfer without asking.
.TP
.B \-\-once
Accept one transfer, then exit.
.SS Options for either mode
.TP
.B \-\-no\-encrypt
Send file contents unencrypted. Slightly faster on a network you trust;
refused when the transfer would have to go through the server.
.TP
.B \-\-encrypt
Encrypt file contents (the default).
.TP
.B \-\-verify
Hash every file and compare. Costs an extra read pass on both sides. Off by
default: in encrypted mode each chunk's authentication tag already proves its
bytes.
.TP
.BR \-\-server = HOST [: PORT ]
Use a different rendezvous server for this run.
.TP
.BR \-\-force\-transport = T
Insist on one path:
.BR tcp ,
.B udp
or
.BR relay .
Without this, drop-zone tries all three in that order.
.TP
.BR \-\-config\-dir = DIR
Use a different configuration directory.
.TP
.BR \-v ", " \-\-verbose
Show transport attempts (TCP, UDP, relay) and other connection details.
Without this, drop-zone still reports errors, a wrong public password,
and accepting or rejecting files, but hides the transport ladder.
.TP
.BR \-q ", " \-\-quiet
Only report problems.
.TP
.BR \-\-log\-level = LEVEL
.BR error ,
.BR warn ,
.B info
or
.BR debug .
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Print a help message.
.TP
.BR \-V ", " \-\-version
Print the version.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I $XDG_CONFIG_HOME/drop-zone/config.toml
Username, server address, preferences. No secrets. Mode 0600.
.TP
.I $XDG_CONFIG_HOME/drop-zone/identity.key
Ed25519 identity, sealed under the private password. Mode 0600.
.TP
.I $XDG_CONFIG_HOME/drop-zone/known_peers
Trust-on-first-use pins, one username per line.
.PP
.B DROP_ZONE_HOME
overrides the configuration directory. Used by the end-to-end test to run two
clients on one machine.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B DROP_ZONE_HOME
Configuration directory.
.TP
.B DROP_ZONE_ALLOW_LOOPBACK
If set, include 127.0.0.1 in the candidate list. Required for a loopback test;
useless in real use, because a peer cannot reach our loopback.
.SH SEE ALSO
Full protocol, security and performance notes live in the source tree under
.IR docs/ .
.SH AUTHORS
drop-zone
